  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon (web)\n\nThis is a small (80 files), deliberately server-component-heavy Next.js 16 app: almost every screen reads Postgres directly in a Server Component, and the *only* client-side data fetching in the whole app is the run-status poller (`run-live.tsx`). That architecture already rules out most of the classic anti-patterns you asked about. Below is an honest breakdown — clean categories are marked as such, and every real finding has a file:line and a concrete fix.\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none found\n\nEvery list/detail read in `lib/server/*.ts` either joins in one query (`listRuns`, `getRunDetail` in `lib/server/runs.ts:51-79` join `sources` twice via `aliasedTable` + `rulesets`, all in a single `select`) or batches with `inArray`/`Promise.all` instead of looping (`findSourceIds` in `lib/server/sources.ts:41-50`, the `Promise.all([findSourceIds(...), getRuleset(...)])` in `app/api/runs/route.ts:37-40`, and `buildRunExport`'s parallel `loadDecisions`/`loadSideAAccounts` in `lib/server/export.ts:90-93`). No per-row queries inside a `.map()`/loop anywhere in the server layer.\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders — one real spot\n\nAlmost everything renders server-side, so there's very little client state to over-render. The one issue is in `components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:61,166-179`:\n\n```ts\nconst ordered = orderRulesByEvaluation(rules);   // re-sorts on every render\n...\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);             // O(n) scan, once per row → O(n²)\n  return <RuleRow key={rule.id} position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1} ... />; // another O(n) scan\n```\n\nEvery keystroke in *any* field re-sorts the whole rule list and does two linear scans per row to recover the index. Small `n` today, but it's pure waste since `rules` already carries a stable `id`. Fix — memoize the sort and drop `indexOf` in favor of a lookup built once:\n\n```ts\nconst ordered = useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules]);\nconst indexById = useMemo(\n  () => new Map(rules.map((r, i) => [r.id, i])),\n  [rules],\n);\n// ordered.map((rule, position) => ... indexById.get(rule.id) ...)\n```\n\nEverywhere else (`run-table.tsx`, `source-table.tsx`, `new-run-form.tsx`) is either static server output or already correctly memoized (`crmCandidates`/`warehouseCandidates` via `useMemo` in `new-run-form.tsx:28-35`).\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n- `listSources()` and `listRulesets()` hit Postgres fresh on every navigation to `/sources`, `/rules`, `/runs/new` — by design (the comment in `sources.ts:14-18` explicitly opts out of build-time caching via `connection()` because this data is \"request-time state, not build-time\"). Given writes are human-driven and rare, this is a reasonable place to add `unstable_cache` with tag invalidation if DB latency ever shows up in practice:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { unstable_cache } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSources = unstable_cache(\n  async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n    const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n    return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n  },\n  [\"sources-list\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n```\n```ts\n// app/api/sources/route.ts, after createSource()\nimport { revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\nrevalidateTag(\"sources\");\n```\n\nI'd treat this as low-priority, though — it trades a well-understood \"always correct, one round trip\" model for cache-invalidation surface area, in an app whose whole design philosophy (per `AGENTS.md`/inline comments) is \"no client cache, read Postgres directly, stay simple.\" Only worth it if you have a measured latency problem.\n- `RunLive`'s TanStack Query setup (`components/query-provider.tsx:14-31`) is correctly scoped — `staleTime: 0` and `refetchOnWindowFocus: false` are intentional given it's a 2s poll, and the `QueryProvider` is mounted at the one leaf that needs it rather than the root. Nothing to change here.\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nI grepped every component for `setInterval`/`setTimeout`/`addEventListener`/`useEffect` — the only interval in the codebase is the elapsed-time clock in `run-live.tsx:101-108`, and it's cleaned up correctly:\n\n```ts\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const tick = () => setElapsed(Date.now() - startedAt.getTime());\n  tick();\n  if (!running) return;\n  const id = setInterval(tick, 1_000);\n  return () => clearInterval(id);   // cleaned up on unmount and on `running` flip\n}, [running, startedAt]);\n```\n\nEngine HTTP calls (`lib/server/engine.ts:56-61,96-98`) all use `AbortSignal.timeout(...)`, so a slow/dead engine can't leak an open request. The DB client is cached on `globalThis` specifically to avoid opening a new pool connection on every HMR reload in dev (`lib/db/index.ts:22-25`).\n\n### 5. Redundant computations — two small, real ones\n\n**`lib/server/sources.ts:24-31`** — `countSourcesBySide` calls `listSources()` (which already Zod-parses every row) and then does three separate `.filter()` passes over the result:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nCollapse to a single pass:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return all.reduce(\n    (acc, s) => {\n      if (s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\") acc.crm++;\n      else if (s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\") acc.warehouse++;\n      else if (s.kind === \"synthetic\") acc.synthetic++;\n      return acc;\n    },\n    { crm: 0, warehouse: 0, synthetic: 0 },\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**`components/runs/run-timeline.tsx:25-30`** — `detailSuffix(entry)` is invoked twice per audit entry (once to check truthiness, once to render):\n\n```tsx\n{detailSuffix(entry) ? (\n  <>...<span>{detailSuffix(entry)}</span></>\n) : null}\n```\n\nCompute once:\n\n```tsx\nconst suffix = detailSuffix(entry);\n...\n{suffix ? (<>...<span>{suffix}</span></>) : null}\n```\n\nBoth are cheap today (source/audit lists are small), but they're free fixes and the pattern (re-deriving the same value twice, rescanning an already-parsed array) is worth catching before the tables grow.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** this codebase doesn't have the performance debt the prompt was fishing for — the server-component-first architecture, real SQL joins, and disciplined polling already avoid N+1s, re-render storms, and leaks. The five items above are the only genuine, actionable findings; everything else checked out clean.\n",
